   "id": "D0_PREFIX_BROKEN",
   "opcode_pattern": "D0",
   "title": "TLCS-900 D0 prefix mis-emitted for word register ops — D0..D7 is the WORD MEMORY family, not a word register-direct prefix",
   "category": "cpu_silicon",
   "severity": "crash",
   "symptoms": [
    "Hang / watchdog reset",
    "Corrupt register values"
   ],
   "root_cause": "MIS-DECODE, not a silicon fault (HW-confirmed 2026-07-03). 0xD0..0xD7 is a WORD MEMORY addressing family (e.g. `D0 B6 3F 50 00` = `cpw (0xB6),0x0050`). Word register-DIRECT is 0xD8..0xDF. The crashes below happened because the toolchain emitted a D0-prefixed byte intending a word register-immediate/ALU op, but the CPU decodes D0 as a memory access — so the operand bytes are consumed as an address and execution derails. The correct classification is toolchain mis-emission (avoid emitting D0 for register ops), not a broken opcode on silicon.",
   "affected_instructions": [
    "cpl WA (D0 06) — toolchain intended reg-direct; CPU sees a D0 memory op",
    "neg WA (D0 07)",
    "sll N,WA (D0 EE)",
    "inc WA (D0 61) — any inc emitted on the D0 pattern",
    "ldcw dmacN,wa",
    "Any word register op the toolchain wrongly encodes with a D0 prefix (should be D8 reg-direct / E8 long)"
   ],
   "fixes": [
    {
     "op": "cpl WA",
     "replacement": "push WA; ld WA,65535; pop HL; xor A,L; xor W,H"
    },
    {
     "op": "neg WA",
     "replacement": "push WA; ld WA,0; pop HL; sub A,L; sbc W,H"
    },
    {
     "op": "sll N,WA",
     "replacement": "extz XWA; sll N, XWA (D8 prefix safe)"
    },
    {
     "op": "inc WA",
     "replacement": "ld BC,1; add A,C; adc W,B"
    },
    {
     "op": "ldcw dmacN,wa",
     "replacement": "extz xwa; ldcl dmacN, xwa (E8 long/32-bit form; D8 is the word/16-bit prefix)"
    }
   ],
   "notes": [
    "D1..D7 standalone are SAFE (CC900 uses them in production) — because they are WORD MEMORY forms, not register-direct.",
    "HW-confirmed 2026-07-03: the whole D0..D7 range is the WORD MEMORY family; there is no word register-direct form there. Word reg-direct = D8..DF, long reg-direct = E8..EF.",
    "The historic 'D0..D7 silicon-broken' label was a mis-decode/mis-emission, not a silicon defect. Fixes remain valid: never emit D0 for a word register op — use D8 (word) or E8 (long).",
    "Disassembler re-decode of the D0..D7 memory family is in progress; the emitted `!BROKEN D0..D7` warnings reflect the current (pre-re-decode) state."
   ],
   "validated_on_hw": true,
   "validation_ref": "hello_j7, j10, j11 (2026-03-22 → 2026-03-24); reclassified as mis-decode 2026-07-03"
  },
